A||||11||fiero56||I've got two cams selected, which one||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||03-24-2001||06:46 PM||||I'm in the final planning stages for my 2.8 rebuild(actually, this is the final choice), and I haven't been able to decide.<BR>This engine will most likely be a daily driver, so I don't want anything wildly aggressive, and I want to maintain some good useable torque down low(I love low end torque). Here are the specs of the two(both are hydraulic flat tappet):<P><BR>1.)rpm operating range: 500-4,500<BR>Duration advertised:<BR>In: 240   Ex: 248<BR>Duration at .050"<BR>In: 192  Ex: 200<BR>Lobe lift: .260"<BR>Lobe separation: 108*<P>2.)rpm operating range: 800-4,800<BR>Duration advertised:<BR>In: 252  Ex: 252<BR>Duration at .050"<BR>In: 206  Ex: 206<BR>Lobe lift: .280"<BR>Lobe separation: 110*<P>I am leaning towards #2, but want to make sure the engine will not have a lope(or very much of one), and want to make sure I don't lose very much low end power.<P>Both of these cams are in the High Energy series by CompCams. #1 is grind #240H, #2 is grind #252H. <BR>I will be using the stock 1.52:1 rocker ratio.<P>Anybody have any experience with either of these cams? I will be talking with Comp again Monday, but wonder what some of the more experienced members have to say.||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||03-24-2001||06:55 PM||||Those valve lift specs don't seem right, but assuming the duration numbers are correct, both of those cams are fairly mild. One consideration is whether you have a stick shift or an auto. The bigger cams work best with manual trannies. Make sure those cams are designed for computer controlled engines, if you're still using the ECM. Have you checked into the Crane CompuCams or the GM HO cam?||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||03-24-2001||06:58 PM||||Silly me, you didn't list valve lift, only lobe lift. Go with #2 if those are your choices, #1 is fairly close to stock. BTW, wazzup?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||03-24-2001||07:02 PM||||Bats:<BR>I listed lobe lift, calcualated from valve lift numbers. I can supply if needed. <P>Will be going with my 5-speed. Neither are SEMA approved, but I don't care. They are both compatible with ECM. I thought about Crane, but Comp seems so much more customer oriented, they've got my business. Never looked at GM cam before, but I assume they are more $$$. I am looking at just over $300 for a complete (k)kit:<BR>lifters<BR>matched springs<BR>retainers<BR>locks<BR>valve seals<BR>timing chain set||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||03-24-2001||07:13 PM||||I really need to break down and get a dyno program. <P>Valve lift on #1 with 1.5 rocker is .390<BR>Valve lift on #2 with 1.5 rocker is .425<P>Both cams are SEMA #3, but don't expect any problems. <P>This started out as a quick and cheap replace the bottom end while it's out. Turned into a complete rebuild. I will be replacing everything except block, heads, intake and connecting rods. Still cheaper than buying rebuilt so far, but it's adding up. I've got about 2 months to order and get it together before I lose my space, so I've gotta get moving on it. Plus, I still have brake and suspenion work planned on my Formula.<P>Batboy: how is that monster engine you had planned for your truck going?||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-24-2001||07:29 PM||||I am sorry to report I haven't done a ding-dang thing to any of my cars all winter. I've been temporarily reassigned at work and have been busier than a one legged man in an a$$ kicking contest. But, soon my friend, soon...<P>Yes, go with #2, higher than stock lift and fairly short duration, perfect low to mid range torque. I like Comp Cams, great product.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||03-24-2001||07:36 PM||||Good thing you got that Grand Prix then. Sounds like you don't have anytime to spend fixing the '85 or the 'burb.<BR>How do you like that car anyway? Still enjoying it, or is it getting boring?||||reg||1
Z||000007||N_Man||03-24-2001||07:58 PM||||Go with the bigger of the two,  I have the compucam 2030 and still have more then adequate low end.<P>actually in my neck of the woods the GM performance cams are cheaper then the crane BUT i didnt know till after...||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||03-24-2001||09:58 PM||||Funny you should ask about the Grand Prix. I'm still maddly in love with it. The honeymoon ain't over yet. Just last Thursday on the way back from lunch with three other coworkers in the car, a flashy sporty ricemobile chirped the tires next to me at the stoplights as it turned green. One of the ladies yelled, "Race him!"<P>Well I was already rolling and got a crappy start, but we ran perfectly nose to nose all the way to 75 mph. The ricemobile was screaming at very high rpm. Doggone it, we were 20 mph over the speed limit and I had three ladies in the car and this guy was obviously not giving up, so common sense finally prevailed and I shut my 98 GP GT down. No reason to get a drag racing ticket or risk an accident with passengers in the car just because I was acting stupid.<P>A half mile down the road I pulled up behind him at another light. Holy cow! It was a 2000 Toyota Celica GT-S! Those things have that new high tech 180 hp 7,800 rpm engine and probably weighs at least 1,000 pounds less than me. Probably cost close to three times what I paid for my Grand Prix.<P>Had I known it was a Celica GT-S, I would never of ran against him. I have heard those things were fast. The driver would not even look our way at the lights (hehehe). Bet that car was back in at the dealership the next day with that guy screaming at the service manager to fix his car because some big ol' American Pontiac with 4 people inside almost smoked him. Gotta love it!||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||03-25-2001||01:23 AM||||Don't get any of those camshafts if you want a significant improvement over the stock camshaft. A stock 2.8-liter engine with ported manifolds will make about 141 horsepower @ 4500 rpm and 186 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. That will make your fiero go 0-60 mph in 7.62 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.56 seconds @ 85.6 mph<P> The Comp Cams 252-H in the same motor will make about 142 horsepower @ 4500 rpm and 184-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3000 rpm. With this camshaft you car will go 0-60 mph in 7.55 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.51 seconds @ 86 mph.<P> I recommend that you get the Crane Cams PowerMax H-260-2 Camshaft. It will make 155 horsepower @ 5000 rpm and 183-ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm in that same motor. That is good enough to get your fiero to 60 mph in 7.11 seconds and run the quarter mile in 15.2 seconds @ 89 mph. This camshaft is less aggressive than the Crane 2030 so your engine should idle fine. <BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||88formula||03-25-2001||01:25 AM|||| All those specifications are with a five speed Getrag transmission.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
